{
 "id": "wassail-meters-fixture",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": false,
 "title": "Here We Come A-wassailing (metre-change fixture)",
 "description": "A test fixture from the parked arrangement: verse 1 in 6/8, two chorus phrases in 4/4, verse 2 begun in 6/8.",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "beginner",
 "tempo": 90,
 "key": "E",
 "timeSignature": "6/8",
 "pickupBeats": 0,
 "credits": {
  "words": "Traditional English (as printed by Bramley and Stainer, 1871)",
  "tune": "Traditional, Yorkshire (as printed by Bramley and Stainer, 1871)",
  "arrangement": "Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: traditional (the book's index gives the words' source as \"Traditional\"); the book's words editor Henry Ramsden Bramley died 1917",
  "tune": "public domain: traditional (the index gives the air as \"Traditional (Yorkshire)\"); the book's music editor John Stainer died 1901",
  "arrangement": "original, written for Stackapella: our own four parts under the printed tune. The book's four-part setting is unsigned (its index names a harmonizer for some carols, as \"Harm. by J. S.\" for I saw three ships, but none for this one), so it was read only to check the chords, and the alto, tenor and bass lines are our own. The tune as printed, except: the verse's last bar (\"seen.\", printed as a bar of 3/8 before the change to common time) is followed by a dotted crotchet's rest, so the chorus starts on a whole bar and everyone breathes before it; the notes before each breath give up three quarters of a beat (\"door\" and \"too\" in verses 2 and 3, \"you,\", \"too,\" and \"year,\" in the chorus, and the chorus's last \"year\" before verses 2 and 3, which also gives up the next verse's upbeat; verse 1's \"green\" is held for a beat and a quarter, where the book's starred quaver is silent), and at \"bless you, and send you\" \"you\" gives up half a beat and \"and\" comes in a quarter beat late; verse 1 leaves out the two notes the book marks as needed only for the other verses; the chorus's crotchet is sung at the verse's dotted crotchet (the book gives no relation) and written in the song's 6/8 beats; breaths written in for beginners. At the very end the alto sings a third above the tune's low E (F#4 to G#4), with the tenor on the fifth, so the last chord is whole at 2, 3 and 4 singers.",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "H. R. Bramley and John Stainer, Christmas Carols New and Old (London: Novello and Company, Limited; New York: Novello, Ewer and Co.; a later printing of the complete collection, its title page naming Sir John Stainer, so 1888 or later; catalogued [1880]), No. XXXVII, \"The Wassail Song\", p. 78 (four parts, Semi-Chorus in 6/8 and Chorus in common time, verse 1 under the music) and p. 79 (verses 2 to 8), and the index, p. 182 (\"Here we come a wassailing (The Wassail Song)\", words \"Traditional\", air \"Traditional (Yorkshire)\"); in E major as printed, written here in E; verses 1, 3 and 8 and the chorus as printed (\"wandering\" sung on two notes, as the 1871 printing hyphenates it \"wander-ing\"), verses 3 and 8 fitted to the notes by us (on the starred notes, as the book says, and \"beg-\" over the two notes verse 1 gives \"was-sail-\"); Harold B. Lee Library copy, read from the scan at archive.org/details/christmascarolsn00lond/page/n83 (verses n84, index n187). Compared with the first printing of the second series, Christmas Carols New and Old (London: Novello, Ewer and Co., 1871, as Hymnary.org dates it), No. XXXVII: the same music and words (there \"neighbour's children\", \"Master\" and \"Mistress\"), scan at hymnary.org/hymn/CCNO1871/37"
 },
